Fig 2. Discusses the block diagram of the proposed system. This proposed smart helmet design integrates various sensors for enhanced rider safety. An MQ-2 gas sensor detects alcohol, a gyroscope detects accidents, and an IR sensor verifies proper helmet placement. GPS and GSM modules offer live tracking and emergency communication, while the GPT API server efficiently takes in commands and potentially personalizes information displayed on the HUD. This information, includes navigation and direction as of now but can be made to display alcohol readings, accident alerts, navigation instructions, and emergency contacts, if using a self-fabricated user-friendly HUD for minimal distraction. The system prioritizes safety certifications, user-friendliness, and ethical data privacy considerations during development and testing.
A.MICROCONTROLLERS 1. ARDUINO MEGA The Arduino Mega is a microcontroller board designed for electronic prototyping and development purposes. It is an extended version of the popular Arduino Uno, offering an increased number of input and output pins, memory resources, and overall capabilities. Manufactured by Arduino LLC, the Mega is based on the ATmega2560 microcontroller, which provides 256 KB of flash memory, 8 KB of SRAM and 4 KB of EEPROM.

2.ESP8266 The ESP8266 is a highly integrated Wi-Fi module that has gained widespread popularity in the realm of Internet of Things (loT) and wireless connectivity. Developed by Espressif Systems, this compact module integrates a Tensilica L106 32-bit microcontroller and a Wi-Fi transceiver, providing a cost-effective solution for embedding Wi-Fi functionality into various electronic devices. The ESP8266 is particularly known for its low power consumption, compact size, and ease of integration.
B. MQ3 GAS SENSOR The proposed in-vehicle navigation system incorporates an alcohol detection gas sensor, similar to the MQ3 sensor used by law enforcement in breathalyzers. This sensor, embedded at the center of the steering wheel within the Internet of Things (loT) framework, continuously measures the driver's alcohol level through exhalation. The system analyzes this level against a pre-programem d "safe" limit, currently set at the Indian legal standard of 0.03% Blood Alcohol Content (BAC) ( 30 microliters of alcohol per 100 milliliters of blood). If the sensor detects alcohol exceeding this limit, it triggers an alarm or buzzer to prevent engine ignition and ensure safe driving conditions. (Ref. [4]). Fig 3 shows concentration of any gas using the Rs/Ro ratio (Rs = sensor resistance in gas, Ro =resistance in clean air) to estimate gas level. which is:
