F. OLED DISPLAY
Unlike traditional displays, OLED displays don't require backlighting, leading to lower power consumption, a crucial factor for battery life in a portable device like a smart helmet. Additionally, high contrast and resolution ensure that critical information remains clear and easily readable even in bright sunlight, minimizing distractions for the rider and promoting safe riding practices.
Fig 7 shows how the OLED display is connected to the NODE MCU which sends input data to display the contents. This OLED is then used to create a heads-up display by placing it at an angle next to a reflector. On which the rider will be able to see the navigation and directions of his waypoint.

