lli.RESULT
This paper presents the development and characterization of a novel smart helmet system designed to enhance rider safety through a multi-sensorial approach. The system integrates a suite of sensors, including an MQ3 gas sensor, a gyroscope sensor, an IR sensor, and a GPS/GSM module, offering unique functionalities and potential safety benefits.

During the testing the MQ3 gas sensor was able to detect alcohol almost 98 out of 100 times, which comes at a 98% accuracy and could possibly lead to at least 10% reduction in alcohol-related accidents, potentially saving 10 lives per 100 accidents.[15]
Moreover in-vehicle navigation systems create distraction risks, known to increase driver reaction times by up to 1.5 seconds [1]. Unlike existing systems that force drivers to look away from the road, this proposed method leverages Augmented Reality (AR) technology in form of a IillD display where the navigation will overlap the user's vision reducing the reaction to almost 0.5 sees.
The GPS/GSM module was able to detect the correct position and send messages to the emergency contacts almost 36 out of 40 times, which results in a 90% accuracy. While the GSM was working as expected the GPS module failed to detect the correct location sometimes when placed inside a closed room or covered with an object.Still when compared to the traditional way of calling the emergency contacts manually this process resulted in 40-50% faster emergency response which could translate to a decrease in the risk of death, potentially saving 8 lives per 10 accidents.[14]
The gyroscope sensor was able to detect accidents and trigger medical assistance, almost 40 out of 40 times, potentially leading to a significant overall reduction in fatalities and a decrease in serious injuries all because the medical help would be provided at the right time.[16]
Also with all the added components the weight of the prototype helmet increased almost 200-250 gm which is one of the application constraints along with the reduction in accuracy of the MQ3 gas sensor if a person is riding with a mask on.
The cost in making can be brought down to �3500 given that we manufacture our own pcb and module.Moreover comprehensive and innovative smart helmet system presents a promising approach to enhancing rider safety and promoting responsible riding practices. The system leverages readily available sensors with established accuracy ranges. Fig 9 shows the entire specification of the prototype helmet model used.

